cratic edge of about three points balances the partisan lean against that evidence.", "watch": "Any independent or Republican-sponsored poll showing De La Cruz back within a few points would signal the market and Democratic-sponsored polls are overstating Pulido's position.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Monica De La Cruz (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican Monica De La Cruz faces Tejano music star Bobby Pulido in a South Texas district that Republicans flipped in 2022 and now carry a seven-point partisan lean into. Pulido has raised nearly $3 million with outside groups supporting him, while De La Cruz holds a cash advantage. Recent polls, mostly from Democratic-leaning sponsors, show Pulido leading by five to thirteen points, and prediction markets price him around an 80 percent chance of winning. Expert raters are split, with several calling it a Tossup and others leaning Republican.\n\nThe race is a test of whether Democratic gains among Latino voters in South Texas can hold. Deportation policy and Democratic outreach to Tejano communities have featured heavily in coverage. The evidence points to a narrow Democratic edge, but the district's lean and the incumbent's resources keep the race within reach for Republicans.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4454, "margin": -0.75}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6234, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6548, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6308, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5679, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6942, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6077, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6479, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.7065, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.6601, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.6541, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.6766, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.7